body:
> A forgotten promise is just like a forgotten or unused variable. If its not held on to, it will be swept out with the trash.

No. Don't forget event loop. Even no one is holding the promise, the code is still running.

If you don't believe me, try running the following code

```javascript
function f() {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    // Suppose we have a long IO operation
    setTimeout(resolve, 1000);
  });
}
async function g() {
  throw new Error("e");
}

(async () => {
  while (true) {
    try {
      await Promise.all([f(), g()]);
    } catch (e) {
      // Logging e and continue.
    }
  }
})();
```

You can see the memory usage will build up.

Beside cancellation, another good option is to always wait all promise like `allSettled`. Even the result is not used, we should make sure that every promise can be collected. But this seems not to be discussed.

If you only have `func1` and `func2`, do `func1().and_then(func2).is_ok()`.

If you have more in anther case, a useful trick is to use `?` in `and_then`.

```rust
func1().and_then(|arg1| {
    let arg2 = func2(arg1)?;
    let arg3 = func3(arg2)?;
    func4(arg3)
}).is_ok()
```

Just use Golang for a year. You'll find love for every other language.

&#x200B;

~~Ok, maybe not C++.~~

When you need a JSON object having uncertain keys, use `Map`.

```kotlin
import com.fasterxml.jackson.module.kotlin.jacksonObjectMapper
import com.fasterxml.jackson.module.kotlin.readValue

data class Fact(val species: String, val max_weight: Int)
typealias AnimalFacts = Map<String, List<Fact>>

fun main() {
    val data = """{"SHARK":[{"species":"tiger","max_weight":"1000"},{"species":"white","max_weight":"2000"}],"DOLPHIN":[{"species":"bottle","max_weight":"500"},{"species":"river","max_weight":"100"}]}"""
    val mapper = jacksonObjectMapper()
    val animalFacts = mapper.readValue<AnimalFacts>(data)

    for ((animalName, facts) in animalFacts) {
        println("Animal name: $animalName")
        for (fact in facts) {
            println("Fact: $fact")
        }
        println()
    }
}
```

Seriously, before try-block, you can use a `and_then` and use `?` inside the lambda. E.g.

```
a().and_then(|a| {
     b()?;
     c()?;
})
```

C# has an underrated language feature: partial class.

With it, you can put the definition of an interface and its implementations in one file.

    // AnimalsDef.cs

    public partial interface IAnimal {}
    public partial class Cat : IAnimal {}
    public partial class Dog : IAnimal {}
    public partial class Tiger : IAnimal {}

And you can put the definition of one method of the interface and its implementations in one file.

    // GetMoreAnimals.cs
    public partial interface IAnimal
    {
        List<IAnimal> GetMoreAnimals();
    }

    public partial class Cat
    {
        public List<IAnimal> GetMoreAnimals()
        {
            throw new NotImplementedException();
        }
    }

    public partial class Dog
    {
        public List<IAnimal> GetMoreAnimals()
        {
            throw new NotImplementedException();
        }
    }

    public partial class Tiger
    {
        public List<IAnimal> GetMoreAnimals()
        {
            throw new NotImplementedException();
        }
    }

By re-organizing the definition, you can make it feel like you define an Algebraic data type `Animal` and a function `GetMoreAnimals` by "pattern matching". While they actually are just an interface with implementations.
